<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Written Homework Exercises</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Every Wednesday at 11:59 pm, 5 exercises will be due.</alert>
      This collection of exercises will grow, with 5 exercises added each week.
      You will need to regularly look at the updated version of this page on Blackboard.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>

    <!-- Q1 Section 1.1 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          For each of the given systems, state (i) the number of equations, (ii) the number of unknowns, and (iii) whether
or not the system is linear. For (iii), provide written justification for your conclusion.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>
          \begin{cases}
x_1 \amp - \amp 3x_2 \amp + \amp 4x_3 \amp - \amp x_4 \amp = \amp 2 \\
x_1 \amp - \amp 2x_2  \amp + \amp x_3 \amp \ \amp \ \amp = \amp 5 \\
\end{cases}
</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

<task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>
          \begin{cases}
x \amp - \amp 5y \amp + \amp 2z \amp = \amp 2 \\
3x \amp + \amp 2y \amp - \amp 4z \amp = \amp 7 \\
9x \amp + \amp 3y \amp - \amp z \amp = \amp -4 \\
-2x \amp - \amp y \amp + \amp 8z \amp = \amp 0 \\
\end{cases}
</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>
          \begin{cases}
x^2 \amp - \amp 2xy \amp + \amp y^3 \amp = \amp 9 \\
\sin(x) \amp + \amp e^y \amp - \amp 4y \amp = \amp 0 \\
\end{cases}
</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>

<!-- Q2 Section 1.2 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Give an example of a matrix in reduced row echelon form that describes 
          a linear system having the stated properties.  For each, write one short 
          sentence that explains why your example meets the stated criteria. If it 
          is not possible to find such an example, explain why not.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          a linear system having 4 equations and 2 unknowns and having exactly one solution.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

        <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          a linear system having 3 equations and 3 unknowns and having no solution.
           </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

       <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          a linear system having 2 equations and 5 unknowns and having infinitely many solutions.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

<task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          a linear system having 3 equations and 5 unknowns and having exactly one solution.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          a linear system having 4 equations and 4 unknowns and having exactly one solution.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>

    <!-- Q3 Section 1.2 -->
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A given linear system has been represented by an augmented matrix and the process of Gaussian 
          elimination has begun.  The current state of the process is shown in the augmented matrix below.
             </p>
             <p>
              Execute the next <alert>three steps</alert> (that is, the next three row operations) of Gaussian elimination, 
              and clearly state the three row operations you used, employing notation like we've been using in class.  
              Your work should show at least two additional matrices beyond the one stated here. 
              <md>
              \left[
    \begin{array}{rrr|r}
      1 \amp 5 \amp -3 \amp 6 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -4 \\
      0 \amp -2 \amp 7 \amp 0 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]
              </md>
              </p>
        </statement>

    </exercise>

<!-- Q4 Section 1.2 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Consider the linear system:
          <md>
          \begin{array}{rcrcrcr}
-2x_1 \amp + \amp x_2 \amp \ \amp \ \amp = \amp -4 \\
3x_1 \amp \ \amp \  \amp + \amp 3x_3 \amp = \amp 9 \\
-x_1 \amp - \amp 2x_2  \amp - \amp 5x_3 \amp = \amp -7
\end{array}
          </md>
          </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the augmented matrix that corresponds to the given system.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

        <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Work by hand to find the reduced row echelon form of the augmented matrix.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

       <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Identify which variables are basic and which are free.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

<task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe the solution space of the system, using a parametric description if there are infinitely many solutions.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>

    <!-- Q5 Section 1.3 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          For each of the following linear systems of equations, (i) represent the system with an augmented matrix, 
          (ii) use \emph{Sage} to find the RREF of the matrix of the linear system, and report the RREF in your written
           summary, and (iii) describe the solution space of the system in as much detail as you can, using parametric 
           form if there are infinitely many solutions.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>
          \begin{cases}
x \amp - \amp 5y \amp + \amp 2z \amp = \amp 2 \\
3x \amp + \amp 2y \amp - \amp 4z \amp = \amp 7 \\
9x \amp + \amp 3y \amp - \amp z \amp = \amp -4 \\
-2x \amp - \amp y \amp + \amp 8z \amp = \amp 0  \\
\end{cases}
</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

<task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>
          \begin{cases}
x_1 \amp - \amp 3x_2 \amp + \amp 4x_3 \amp - \amp x_4 \amp = \amp 2 \\
x_1 \amp - \amp 2x_2 \amp + \amp x_3 \amp \ \amp \ \amp = \amp 5 \\
\end{cases}
</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>
          \begin{cases}
x_1 \amp - \amp 3x_2 \amp + \amp 4x_3 \amp - \amp x_4 \amp = \amp 2 \\
x_1 \amp - \amp 2x_2  \amp + \amp x_3 \amp \ \amp \ \amp = \amp 5 \\
-2x_1 \amp + \amp 1x_2  \amp - \amp 3x_3 \amp + \amp x_4 \amp = \amp -1 \\
-x_1 \amp - \amp 4x_2  \amp + \amp 2x_3 \amp + \amp 5x_4 \amp = \amp 0 \\
\end{cases}
</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>


    <!-- Q6 Section 1.4 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          For each of the four scenarios below, give an example of an 
          augmented matrix in RREF that satisfies the stated criteria, or explain 
          why such a matrix is not possible.  
          </p>
      <p>
      For each example you provide, (i) circle the pivot locations, (ii) make each 
      non-pivot column have at least one non-zero entry, and (iii) write one sentence 
      to say why the system is consistent or inconsistent.  In addition, if the corresponding 
      linear system is consistent, describe the solution space for your example.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An augmented matrix with four rows, three columns, and two pivots for which the corresponding linear system is consistent.  
      </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

        <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An augmented matrix with three rows, five columns, and three pivots for which the corresponding linear system is inconsistent. 
             </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

       <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An augmented matrix that corresponds to a linear system of 4 equations in 3 variables that has infinitely many solutions.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

<task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An augmented matrix that corresponds to a linear system of 3 equations in 5 unknown variables that has a unique solution.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>

    <!-- Q7 Section 2.1 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Consider the vectors <m>\uvec</m>, <m>\vvec</m>, <m>\xvec</m>, <m>\yvec</m>, 
          and <m>\zvec</m> pictured in this figure:
          </p>
       <image source="images/HW-lin-comb-nonstd.eps" width="50%">
                <shortdescription>
                    4 vectors in a plane.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

      <p>
      Notice that while there is no scale on the axes, the vectors 
      <m>\uvec</m> and <m>\vvec</m> generate a "grid", and the tips of the 
      vectors <m>\xvec</m>, <m>\yvec</m>, and <m>\zvec</m> lie at intersection points 
      on the grid.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each of the vectors <m>\xvec</m>, <m>\yvec</m>, and <m>\zvec</m>, express the vector 
          as a linear combination of <m>\uvec</m> and <m>\vvec</m>.  
          Clearly state each linear combination by writing an equation such 
          as <m>\xvec = \underline{\hspace{0.25in}}\uvec +  \underline{\hspace{0.25in}}\vvec</m>.  
          After you do this for all three vectors, write a sentence that explains how you determined 
          the weights in your linear combinations.	 	
      </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

        <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch and label the vector  <m>\wvec = 3\uvec - \vvec</m>.
             </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

       <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What geometric figure is formed by the tips of all vectors of the form 
          <m>{\uvec + t\vvec}</m>, when <m>t</m> is allowed to be any real number?  
          (Hint: you might plot some points that correspond to several different 
          values of <m>t</m> that you choose.)  Write a careful sentence or two that explains 
          your conclusion and how you determined it.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>


    <!-- Q8 Section 2.1 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Consider the linear system 
	<md>
\begin{array}{rcrcr}
2x \amp - \amp 3y \amp = \amp 13 \\
3x \amp + \amp y  \amp = \amp 3 \\
\end{array}
</md>
</p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system and describe the solution space fully.   
          If there are infinitely many solutions, describe them in 
          parametric form.	
      </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

        <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\uvec = \twovec{2}{3}</m> and <m>\vvec = \twovec{-3}{1}</m>.  
         How can you rewrite the given linear system above as asking 
         a question about a linear combination of <m>\uvec</m> and <m>\vvec</m>?
             </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

       <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
         Let <m>\bvec = \twovec{13}{3}</m>.  Is <m>\bvec</m> a linear combination of 
         <m>\uvec</m> and <m>\vvec</m>?  Why or why not?  If yes, give the weights 
         that make <m>\bvec</m> a linear combination of <m>\uvec</m> and <m>\vvec</m>; 
         if no, explain why no such weights exist.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>

<!-- Q9 Section 2.2 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Suppose that <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 2</m> matrix whose columns are the 
          vectors <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>: <m>A = [ \vvec_1 \ \ \vvec_2]</m>.
</p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What are the dimensions of the vectors <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>?	
      </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

        <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the matrix-vector product <m>A \twovec10</m> as a linear combination of <m>\vvec_1</m> 
          and <m>\vvec_2</m>.  Similarly, write <m>A \twovec01</m> as a linear combination of <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
             </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

       <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
         Suppose we are now given some specific information about certain 
         matrix-vector products.  Specifically, say we are given
		<md>A \twovec10 = \threevec{2}{-1}{4} \ \mbox{and} \ A \twovec01 = \threevec{0}{3}{-1}.</md>
		What is the matrix <m>A</m>?  Why?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
         Determine <m>A \twovec{-3}{5}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>

    <!-- Q10 Section 2.3 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Consider the vectors <m>\uvec = \threevec{1}{0}{-1}</m>, <m>\vvec = \threevec{0}{-1}{1}</m>, and <m>\wvec = \threevec{2}{-3}{1}</m>.
</p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is <m>\bvec = \threevec{-5}{7}{-3}</m> in Span<m>\{ \uvec, \vvec, \wvec \}</m>?  Why or why not?
      </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

        <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is <m>\cvec = \threevec{-5}{7}{-2}</m> in Span<m>\{ \uvec, \vvec, \wvec \}</m>?  Why or why not?
             </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

       <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
         Do the vectors <m>\uvec, \vvec, \wvec</m> span all of <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>?  
         Why or why not?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
         What is an important observation to make about the relationship 
         among <m>\uvec, \vvec, \wvec</m>?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
         Give a careful written description of Span<m>\{ \uvec, \vvec, \wvec \}</m>.	
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>

    <!-- Q11 Section 2.3 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Suppose that <m>A</m> is the matrix  <m>A = [ \vvec_1 \ \ \vvec_2 \ \ \vvec_3]</m> where 
	<md>
	\vvec_1 = \threevec111, \ \ \vvec_2 = \threevec{\phantom{-}2}{-1}{\phantom{-}0}, \ \ \vvec_3 = \threevec{-1}{\phantom{-}3}{\phantom{-}2}
	</md>
  </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>\xvec = \threevec{\phantom{-}4}{-2}{\phantom{-}3}</m>, write <m>A\xvec</m> in two different ways:  
          (i) as a linear combination of <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>, and <m>\vvec_3</m>, 
          and (ii) as a single vector that you find by hand.
      </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

        <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>\bvec = \threevec{\phantom{-}5}{-7}{-3}</m>, determine all 
          solutions to the equation <m>A\xvec = \bvec</m>.  Clearly show 
          how this problem can be turned into a system of equations 
          (don't just write down an augmented matrix), and solve. If there are 
          infinitely many solutions, express them in parametric vector form.
             </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

       <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
         Is <m>\cvec =  \threevec{\phantom{-}5}{-7}{-2}</m> a linear 
         combination of <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>, and <m>\vvec_3</m>?
           Clearly show how this question can be turned into a system of 
           linear equations and then explain your answer.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>

    <!-- Q12 Section 2.3 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Suppose that we have the following collection of vectors in 
          <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m>:  <md>{\vvec_1 = \fourvec{1}{1}{1}{1}, 
          \vvec_2 = \fourvec{\phantom{-}1}{\phantom{-}1}{-1}{-1}, 
          \vvec_3 = \fourvec{\phantom{-}1}{-1}{\phantom{-}1}{-1}}</md>.
  </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give an example of a nonzero vector $\wvec$ that is not equal 
          to <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>, or <m>\vvec_3</m> that belongs to the 
          span of <m>\{ \vvec_1, \vvec_2, \vvec_3 \}</m>.  Write a sentence 
          to say why your vector has the desired property.

      </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

        <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why Span<m>\{ \vvec_1, \vvec_2, \vvec_3 \}</m> is not 
          <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m> using pivots.
             </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

       <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
         Find a vector <m>\vvec_4</m> so that <m>\vvec_4</m> is not in 
         Span<m>\{ \vvec_1, \vvec_2, \vvec_3 \}</m>, and clearly justify 
         why your vector has this property.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
         What can you say about Span<m>\{ \vvec_1, \vvec_2, \vvec_3, \vvec_4 \}</m>?  Why?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>


  </page>
</worksheet>
